## Concept Overview
Dynamic programming optimizes recursive problems by caching subproblem results. In React, useMemo prevents expensive recalculations during re-renders.

## Implementation Details
### Top-Down Memoization (Recursive)
```javascript
const fibonacci = useMemo(() => {
  const memo = {};
  const fib = (n) => {
    if (n in memo) return memo[n];
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    memo[n] = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
    return memo[n];
  };
  return fib;
}, []);
```

### Bottom-Up Tabulation (Iterative)
```javascript
const fibonacci = useMemo(() => {
  return (n) => {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    let prev = 0, curr = 1;
    for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
      [prev, curr] = [curr, prev + curr];
    }
    return curr;
  };
}, []);
```

## Common Pitfalls
- **Dependency array**: Empty array [] ensures function isn't recreated
- **Memory leaks**: Large memo objects can cause memory issues
- **Over-optimization**: useMemo overhead may exceed benefits for simple calculations
- **Referential equality**: useMemo preserves function reference for child components

## Why This Is Asked

Concurrency control in the browser is critical for perf and stability. A closed, bounded queue surfaces realistic debugging and trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Closure-based concurrency state (queue, active, limit)
- Promise queue with drain logic
- Correct error propagation and pool resilience
- Real-world cap (4) and dynamic scaling

## Code Example

```javascript
function createQueue(limit) {
  const q = [];
  let active = 0;
  const next = () => {
    if (active >= limit || q.length === 0) return;
    const { task, resolve, reject } = q.shift();
    active++;
    Promise.resolve()
      .then(() => task())
      .then(resolve)
      .catch(reject)
      .finally(() => {
        active--;
        next();
      });
  };
  return {
    enqueue: (task) => new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      q.push({ task, resolve, reject });
      next();
    })
  };
}
```

